From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chad Cormier Roussel <chadcormierroussel@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 07:02:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Fill the ARM64EC auxiliary IAT and revert an entry
 when what it was resolved through is made writable.

An ARM64EC import call loads the importer's auxiliary IAT entry. Nothing wrote it, so it kept the import's check stub
(its AuxiliaryIATCopy value) and every call ran 39-67 instructions of checking (the stub, __icall_helper_arm64ec,
arm64x_check_call) where a filled entry runs adrp; ldr; blr.

Once a module's imports are bound, the loader fills each entry whose regular IAT entry leads to EC code: directly, through
an FFS, or through one ff 25 hop to either. The fill mirrors arm64x_check_call, reads only memory inside loaded images,
and keeps the checker for anything else (syscall stubs, allocate_stub's stubs, an alias whose slot isn't bound yet) and
for anything resolved through a page that is writable. Relay and snoop runs keep today's path.

Each filled entry records what its resolution read: the importer's IAT entry, then each code address and ff 25 slot.
A protect that makes one of those pages writable, in this process (the NtProtectVirtualMemory wrapper, after the
protect, so before the hooker writes, and after FEX's post-notify, so no FEX lock is held while it waits) or from
another (a writable post-protect, a flush or a write on the cross-process work list, also after FEX's notify for that
entry; an overflowed list reverts everything), puts that entry back to its AuxiliaryIATCopy stub.
Entries resolved through other pages stay filled. A 65,536-bit page hash keeps a protect elsewhere free of any scan.
LLVM dec0781 places the IAT on its own 4 KiB pages at the start of .rdata so that the OS can detect runtime patching
and revert the auxiliary IAT to call checking; reverting per entry, and following the FFS and ff 25 pages, is ours.

The fill queries each page once, after setting its hash bit, then resolves again, all under aux_lock, so a hooker on
another thread either sees the bit and reverts after the fill, or came before the query and keeps the stub. The query
is the raw syscall: the NtQueryVirtualMemory export is hybrid-patchable, and an x64 hook on it would run under
aux_lock. The fill reads a page only once the query says it is committed and readable, so it skips unreadable pages;
a page made no-access between the query and the read can still fault under the loader lock and aux_lock, and that
fault goes to FEX's access-violation handler, which takes ThreadCreationMutex, so with FEX's untrap waiting on
aux_lock while it holds that mutex a narrow lock cycle remains. The revert's lock-free test is the hash alone: an
entry is counted only after its query.

Every aux_lock holder (the fill, the forget and the revert) holds it as a syscall callback, so a suspend waits for the
doorbell, as it does for FEX's own lock holders: a hooker that freezes every other thread and then protects a page
(MinHook) would otherwise wait forever for a fill it froze. From the cross-process work list FEX runs the revert with
InSimulation set, so leaving the callback there leaves the doorbell to FEX.

An unloaded module's entries are dropped before its view is unmapped.

Gaps: a protect or write from another process takes effect here before FEX next processes the work list (an x64
syscall, a block compile or a context sync; an ARM64EC thread's own syscalls don't drain it), so calls in that window
still skip the hook; delay-load IATs aren't filled; syscall stubs stay on the checker.

Ours and stays local.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
